Subject: EYES: Bates exercises (was Keratotomy)

At 12:43 PM 8/23/93 -0700, Robert Blumen wrote:
>X-Message-Number: #93-8-694
>I recently took a class on the "Bates Method" which is a method
>for improving vision by improving your vision habits.  The essence
>of the method is learning to keep your focus point constantly in
>motion from point to point.

Ahem.  I think the Bates exercises are held in pretty low esteem by most
ophthalmologists; I recall Martin Gardner devoting an entire chapter to
them in his big "debunking" book (the title is something like _Fads and
Fallacies_?).  Not that I'm a huge fan of Martin Gardner (as R.A.W. says,
"I wish I could be as sure of anything as Martin Gardner is of
everything."), but the Bates regime sounds pretty far-fetched to me, too.

>The idea of Bates is that
>poor vision comes about through certain muscles and neuro-kinetic
>patterns becoming to rigid, and that by learning new vision habits,
>the visual system becomes more fluid and relaxed.

By what mechanism, exactly, does the Bates method change the shape of the
eyeball?  Since the eye obeys the laws of optics, changing the shape of the
eyeball is the only way to overcome myopic/hyperopic disorders without some
external correction.  How does the Bates method deal with astigmatism
(deviation of the surface of the lens from approximating a sector of a
sphere)?

>I was recommended the course by my physical therapist, who had not
>taken it herself, but her teacher and her teacher's husband had
>taken it.  Both of them had experienced fairly dramatic gains in
>their vision.  The numbers I remember from the husband were going
>from 20/400 to about 20/60. [...] The instructor claimed that he had gone from
>9 diopters in each eye
>to 20/40 (these are not equivalent metrics, but I think that 20/40
>is about 1 diopter) in ten years.
>robert@brt.com                          | Suite 222

How old were these people?  In middle age, the hardening of the lens tissue
(presbyopia) tends to make the eye less near-sighted and more far-sighted,
so myopic persons would expect to have some lessening of their prescription
during those years anyway.

Are there any peer-reviewed double blind studies of the Bates method (one
group doing real Bates exercises, the other mumbling affirmations or some
such) that indicate that it works?  I am open to persuasion on the basis of
clinical evidence, but all I've ever heard is anecdotal stuff as far as the
Bates exercises go.
