Date: Mon, 23 Aug 93 16:24:03 CDT
From: eder@hsvaic.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: SPACE: DC-X Flies

While SSTO in the vein of the proposed Delta Clipper is a nice
technical goal in a purist's sense, it is by no means the most
economical solution.

Here's my current scenario for lowest capital cost space launch,
subject to modification as new ideas come up:

For payload size and rocket engine development cost reasons,
begin with a horizontal takeoff and landing rocket using H2/O2
and powered by a single RL-10 engine.  For initial launch, 
it is towed by a cable running across some empty part of
the country (supported by rollers on top of towers spaced
several miles apart).  The cable is made of carbon fiber.
The cable is driven by a mechanical drive geared to a set
of railroad locomotives on an existing track (you rent the
use of the locomotives when you have a launch to do).

The locomotives provide 120,000 lb traction each and fractional
g acceleration.  Through the mechanical drive (for example a
gear ratio), this is converted to 30,000-40,000 lb traction
at several g's to accelerate your rocket, which is on the
order of 10,000 lb.  The RL-10 provides on the order of
15,000 lb thrust.

The tow rope is to give you a running start in getting through
the atmosphere.  If you need it, some wing area is attached to
the end of the rope to give you lift like a kite as the line
is drawn in.  The rocket will have some wing of it's own
so it can land.  Because SSTO is so marginal, and small vehicles
have more relative losses than large ones to drag, the boost
is quite helpful.  

The rocket is not designed to reach orbit.  Instead it is designed
to go to about 0.7 of orbit speed, and then eject the payload
with a small kick motor (probably solid) in the initial flights.
At 0.7 of orbit speed, you need to dissipate only half the energy
of an orbital entry to land, so you should be able to use metallic
skin in the vehicle rather than tile over most of the surface.
The nose will probably still be carbon.

What you deliver in the early flights is mostly cable to build
an 'orbital suspension bridge', or 'space bridge' for short.  The
large limit case of such a structure is the 'beanstalk' or
'GEO elevator', where the bottom end is at rest on the ground.
This requires much stronger material than we have now.  You can
build any intermediate size from the 20km tether the Shuttle
tried to deploy on the TSS-1 mission (the flight where the reel
stuck), up to several thousand km long using existing carbon fiber
as the structural material.  All of these want to orient vertically
under gravity because of the gravity gradient around the Earth.

The bottom end of these structures moves more slowly than orbital
speed, roughly 1.5 meters/sec per km you are below the center of
gravity of the structure.  So, for a 2000 km cable, the bottom end
will move 1500 m/s slower than orbit speed.

You put a 'landing platform' at the bottom of the structure, and
then use self-winching 'elevators' to run up and down.  The first
increment of cable in theory increases the payload of the rocket
enough to pay back it's mass in 3.5 flights (by moving the
platform down in velocity, the rocket can use less fuel and carry
more payload).  Allowing for overhead, I use 7 flights as the
mass-payback time.  Even at Shuttle flight rates, this is a mass-
payback in one year, a pretty good deal.  If you have weekly
or daily flight rates for a reuseble rocket, you have a great
deal.  The mass of a space bridge grows exponentially with it's
length, so there is a limit how large you want to build it.  The
length you stop at depends on what rate of return you demand
on the structure and how often you fly there.  A resonable stopping
point is 2 km/s velocity knocked off getting to orbit.  With
the initial boost provided by the 'rail launch :-)', you should
be able to get over 1000 lb of payload with a 10,000 rocket.  You
should be able to get a guy in a space suit to the platform in
that weight budget.  Meantime, you will need an assembly robot that
can be tele-operated to assemble the space bridge.

Using the empirical cost scaling relationships of the serospace
industry, we can estimate the cost of the rocket, etc.  If the
Delta Clipper has a takeoff weight of 1,500,000 lb and a mass
fraction of 15% for hardware, we have 225,000 lb of hardware.  The
small rocket in this system should have a hardware weight of
1500 lb, or 1/150th as much.  Aerospace costs usually scale (for
development) as the 0.75 power of hardware mass, so we expect
the small rocket to cost 1/43 as much as the Delta Clipper to
develop.  I've seen figures of 5 billion for the Delta Clipper,
so this rocket should cost on the order of $120 million.  With
the rail launcher, assembly robot, space bridge, etc, we are looking
at a $200-300 million total program that breaks open access to
space.  That's at standard aerospace development costs.  For an
on-the cheap DCX type project, you should be able to get the
rail launch and small rocket up and flying for a few 10's of millions
(the DCX cost 60 million for a rocket 4 times the size), and be
able to get some non-zero payload to orbit for paying customers
(using a solid kick motor top stage), which can help pay for the
later expansions.

Unfortunately, I'm about 2 orders of magnitude short of the
required capital.  Either I wait 25 years and do really well
on my investments, work on a lower capital start-up system (most
of my efforts now), or find some people to help fund the system.

Dani Eder

