From: Elizabeth Schwartz <betsys@cs.umb.edu>
Subject: INVEST: what to do with just a little money?

So, this is the first time in my life that my income exceeds my output
by more than the cost of a pack of diskettes :-)

I'm currently tucking a couple of hundred dollars a month into a
savings account at roughly 3.5% interest, trying to get a large enough
lump to play with. The state is, in lieu of social security (which we
state workers are exempt from) tucking 8% of my before-tax income into
a fund which I will get back when I leave. The State is also vacuuming
a huge amount of taxes since I have no deductions, shelters, or excuses.

These two pockets of money are my total worldly assets, including
potential retirement, which at age 33 I am starting to think about.

What would you do with a small amount of money? I notice that the
savings bank pays more interest on their straight savings than they do
on a money market fund. I don't have enough yet to get into the big
funds, and I don't have enough to buy stock unless I can buy cheap.
If I bought 20 $50 shares, there's a $25 fee to buy and $25 to sell
plus a surcharge on profitsm, and taxes on the profits, so it's hard
to make money on it unless the stock goes up by a fair bit. 

I don't have the ready cash to buy a house, and the out-of-pocket
expenses exceed the gain in value for quite a few years. I figure I'd
need about $5,000 to buy, and the same available in case of major
repairs. 

So, how would you turn a small amount of money into a medium sized
amount? I'm thinking that I should try to put most of it someplace
stable (money market?) and try some small-scale entrepeneurship
with the leftovers. I have another year of night school and can't
tackle anything ambitious until then (I also can go back to working
for a for-profit corporation after that, which should increase my
benefits substantially!)

Where do you moderate-income extropians have your money?

thanks, Betsy






